[
  "[SCENE: Cam on desk, cards visible if possible]\nJuly 2022. Rainy week at Boy Scout Camp Tesomas, northern Wisconsin.\nWooden picnic table. Two standard 52-card decks. A notebook.\n[FREEFORM: Set the scene physically - the rain on the pavilion roof, the mud, the scouts around you,\nwhy you were thinking about card game design instead of whatever you were supposed to be doing]\nI wanted to design a two-player tactical duel with zero dice and no mid-match shuffles.\nEvery outcome had to come strictly from the cards on the table\nand the deliberate choices each player made.\nThat became Phalanx Duel.\n[SHOW: The two-page quickstart PDF or the phalanxduel.com landing page]\nThis is The Sound Above: Special Presentation. I'm Mike Hall.\nToday we're talking about cards, state machines, and why making rules legible\nis the deepest responsibility of software craftsmanship.\n",
  "[SCENE: Terminal open with Ruby script]\n[DEMO: Show the Ruby combat prototype running - suit interactions, column defense math, damage propagation]\n[SHOW: github.com/phalanxduel or the specific bin/ scripts in the repo]\nTo prove the rules worked, I started where craftsmen start: executable code.\nIn Phalanx Duel, each player controls an eight-card formation.\nTwo ranks across four columns.\nWhen an attack hits a column, damage chews through the front-rank vanguard first,\nthen the rear-rank flank, before it can reach Life Points.\n[FREEFORM: Walk through a sample combat turn in the terminal. Explain what the Ruby output means.\nShow the suit interaction rules. Point out where edge cases showed up during early testing\nand what that told you about the rules design.]\nThe rules were simple enough for two scouts on a soggy bench.\nPrecise enough to run identically in software.\nA working prototype is only the beginning.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show WebSocket diagram or TypeScript server code]\n[DEMO: Show the TypeScript engine structure - the intent handler, the state machine, the action ledger]\nThe real challenge was turning tabletop rules into a live multiplayer architecture.\nMany game architectures make the mistake of trusting the client to resolve moves.\nIn Phalanx Duel, the client never dictates state.\nIt sends player intent over a WebSocket connection.\nThe server validates that intent against current deterministic state,\ncomputes the next transition,\nand records the exact action in an immutable ledger.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about why client-authority is the wrong model for anything where integrity matters.\nConnect it to the same problem in production systems: hidden state, unverifiable transitions,\nno ground truth for what actually happened.]\nWhat a table verifies with physical cards is what the server enforces on every turn.\nThe boundary is the same. The discipline is the same.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show role-safe projection diagram or the network protocol flow]\n[SHOW: Slide or diagram - what Player 1 sees vs Player 2 sees vs Spectator sees]\nDeterminism is necessary but not sufficient.\nAn engine can be 100 percent deterministic while executing completely flawed logic.\nTo bridge that gap: replay fingerprints, action verification hashes, role-safe projections.\nThe authoritative server maintains complete match state, including facedown cards.\nWhen broadcasting updates, it projects only what each role should see.\nPlayer 1 gets their private hand. Player 2 gets theirs.\nSpectators get a time-delayed broadcast.\n[FREEFORM: Connect role-safe projections to information security in distributed production systems.\nTalk about where you've seen the same problem in enterprise contexts: services that broadcast too much,\nor clients that trust themselves to compute state they don't own.]\nThe protocol itself becomes an active guardian of the game's integrity.\n",
  "[SCENE: Back on cam or show SCMC slides]\n[FREEFORM: This act should be mostly improvised. Here are the anchors:]\nIn September 2026 I brought this to Software Craftsmanship McHenry County.\nThe talk: Make the Rules Legible.\n[FREEFORM: Talk honestly about the experiment of building the whole presentation with AI tooling.\nWhat accelerated. What completely fell apart. What the model got confidently wrong about your own talk.\nThe meta-lesson from using AI to build a presentation about AI's limits.\nDon't script this - just tell it as it happened.]\nThe meta-lesson: generative tools accelerate scaffolding dramatically.\nBut without human stewardship and deterministic boundaries, they hallucinate fluently.\nThe model can suggest moves. Only an authoritative engine can verify the truth.\n[PAUSE: let the irony land]\n",
  "[SCENE: Browser - phalanxduel.com ranked ladder]\n[SHOW: phalanxduel.com - the live ranked ladder, current match count if visible]\nPhalanx Duel is live in production today.\nAutomated release pipelines. Containerized deployments. Glicko-2 ranked ladder.\n[SHOW: YouTube playlist - youtube.com/playlist?list=PLHenvAZhJjHo]\n[FREEFORM: Talk about Conrad Hall's walkthrough video - 15 years old, high school sophomore,\nAP Computer Science, co-developer. What it means to watch a kid that age pick up\nrules-as-code and run with it. Keep it brief, keep it genuine.]\nDownload the rules, inspect the engine, play a match: phalanxduel.com\nThe lessons from that Wisconsin picnic table:\nserver authority, deterministic replay, visible boundaries, zero-indirection state transitions.\nThose are the same disciplines we're here to talk about across The Sound Above.\nKeep your rules legible. Keep your boundaries explicit.\nKeep your systems safe to change.\n"
]